WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00.580 --> 00:03.300
All effective communications begin with empathy.

2
00:03.940 --> 00:07.460
You cannot know where you’re going if you don’t understand where you’re coming from.

3
00:08.040 --> 00:11.170
And it’s also important to understand where the other person is coming from.

4
00:11.880 --> 00:13.410
That’s why empathy is so important.

5
00:14.150 --> 00:18.180
At a high level, empathy is the ability to understand — or at least ask —

6
00:18.500 --> 00:21.760
what might the other person be thinking or feeling about this topic?

7
00:22.440 --> 00:23.750
What is important to them?

8
00:24.710 --> 00:29.990
How might what they’re feeling about the topic influence what’s possible in the meeting or in the interaction?

9
00:30.310 --> 00:35.312
The mindset of an effective communicator is one that I always describe as “over there”.

10
00:35.312 --> 00:38.532
Think of it in terms of “over there” versus “over here”.

11
00:38.532 --> 00:43.707
Let’s say, for example, when I’m “over here”, I’m wrapped up thinking about my own agenda,

12
00:43.707 --> 00:47.100
worried about my own timelines, my own goals, my own needs.

13
00:47.300 --> 00:53.630
I’m really caught up in my own inner dialogue and not really taking into account the other person or what they have in mind.

14
00:54.210 --> 01:01.350
But when I’m in an “over there” mindset, I’m actually open and curious and interested in what they may be thinking or feeling about the topic.

15
01:02.020 --> 01:06.270
And therefore we can have a really better, more nuanced conversation,

16
01:06.600 --> 01:10.880
where it’s much more inclusive and engaging and hopefully leads to real action.

17
01:11.370 --> 01:15.500
Great listening is such an important part of being an effective communicator.

18
01:15.500 --> 01:21.830
Great listening is about being present, being genuinely interested and curious about what the other person has to say,

19
01:22.890 --> 01:30.050
asking questions to better understand their perspective, and really listening for what the other person is saying — and maybe not saying.

20
01:30.850 --> 01:32.930
You can even recognise when you’re not doing it.

21
01:33.730 --> 01:36.552
Say you’re rushing between meetings, calls and deadlines.

22
01:36.552 --> 01:41.700
You arrive at your next meeting still processing the last one, and your phone beeps with a text message:

23
01:42.050 --> 01:46.210
“Where’s the report for Janine? She needs it in two hours.” — “In a meeting.”

24
01:46.570 --> 01:49.410
You’re not really listening to what the other person is saying.

25
01:49.770 --> 01:54.630
You may be planning what you want to say next rather than really listening to the other person.

26
01:55.560 --> 02:00.320
People can tell when it is an asynchronous conversation, and that you’re not really present.

27
02:00.900 --> 02:03.360
And at that point, what’s the point of even talking?

28
02:03.720 --> 02:09.220
A lot of times, if you do have even just a minute before a meeting: stop, think and pause.

29
02:09.700 --> 02:11.070
Take a few deep breaths.

30
02:11.720 --> 02:13.730
Focus on thinking about the other person,

31
02:14.410 --> 02:20.580
and remind yourself to keep an open mind as to what the agenda might look like and how the conversation might go.

32
02:21.000 --> 02:26.320
Sometimes that can make it a little bit easier to more naturally fall into an “over there” mindset.

33
02:26.320 --> 02:29.060
We’ve discussed what empathy is and what it is not,

34
02:29.610 --> 02:31.910
and next we will look at why empathy matters.

35
02:32.390 --> 02:35.300
This isn’t the end of the conversation when it comes to empathy.

36
02:35.620 --> 02:40.130
Empathy is going to weave itself throughout this course and in every step of the process,

37
02:40.650 --> 02:50.110
because it’s important that we constantly revisit how our audience — how the other person — is thinking and feeling as we’re creating and developing any kind of interaction.

